# XSH pthread_exit()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# pthread_exit() is never called from a cleanup handler or destructor run
# by thread exit (undefined), and no value points into the exiting
# thread's own automatic storage.

assertion  XSH.pthread_exit.001
anchor     functions/pthread_exit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       pthread_exit() called from a function the start routine calls
           ends the thread there (nothing after the call runs), and
           pthread_join() obtains its argument; two threads exiting with
           different values each give their own.
test       t/001-terminates.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_exit.002
anchor     functions/pthread_exit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.3
class      required
since      2001
says       Cleanup handlers pushed and not yet popped (three, nested) run
           at pthread_exit() last pushed first; one already popped
           without being executed does not run.
test       t/002-cleanup-lifo.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_exit.003
anchor     functions/pthread_exit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.9.5.3
class      required
since      2001
says       At pthread_exit() the cleanup handler runs before the destructor
           of a thread-specific data key the thread set, and still sees
           the key's value; the destructor then gets that value.
test       t/003-cleanup-then-dtors.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_exit.004
anchor     functions/pthread_exit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A thread that ends with a mutex locked and a pipe open leaves
           both as they were (pthread_mutex_trylock() then fails with
           EBUSY; the pipe still carries data), and no atexit() handler
           runs.
note       The mutex has the default attributes, so it is not robust
           (PTHREAD_MUTEX_STALLED) and stays owned.
test       t/004-no-process-cleanup.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_exit.005
anchor     functions/pthread_exit.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       When the initial thread has called pthread_exit() and the last
           other thread then calls pthread_exit((void *)5), the process
           ends as exit(0) would: status exited with 0, an atexit() handler
           has run and a fully buffered stdout was flushed; the other
           thread ran on after the initial thread's call.
note       The process is a new image of the test (fork() then execv()).
test       t/005-last-thread.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_exit.006
anchor     functions/pthread_exit.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fork.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The same holds in a child made by fork() (without exec): after
           its initial thread has called pthread_exit() and its other
           thread then calls pthread_exit((void *)5), the child ends with
           status exited with 0, its atexit() handler has run and its fully
           buffered stdout was flushed.
note       A child that never ends is UNRESOLVED by the timeout: the text
           sets no time by which the process must exit.
timeout    20
test       t/006-last-thread-fork-child.c
